    <h1>Private Label</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Private label is the fastest route from "no product" to "your brand on the shelf." You take an
      existing LanDeng product, apply your label and packaging, and sell it under your name — without
      developing a new formula.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Direct answer:</strong> Private label means you re-brand a manufacturer's
      <em>existing</em> product as your own. It is distinct from OEM, where the manufacturer builds a
      product <em>to your specification</em>. Private label is faster and simpler to launch because the
      product already exists; OEM gives you ownership of the formula and takes longer. See
      <a href="/blog/oem-vs-private-label/">OEM vs private label</a> for the full comparison.
    </div>

    <h2>What we supply</h2>
    <p>
      The private-label program is built on the same product lines as our
      <a href="/wholesale/">wholesale</a> range — Chinese botanical incense made from natural woods,
      resins, herbs, and flowers:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Incense sticks</strong> (线香)</li>
      <li><strong>Incense cones</strong> (塔香) and <strong>coils</strong> (盘香)</li>
      <li><strong>Sachets</strong> (香囊)</li>
      <li><strong>Raw materials</strong> (香材)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The distinction that matters: these are <em>existing</em> products. You are choosing from what we
      already make, then putting your brand on it — you are not commissioning a new scent. If you need a
      specific aroma, ingredient, or format that does not already exist, that is
      <a href="/oem/">OEM</a>, not private label.
    </p>
    <p>
      "Existing" does not mean "uninspected." The material is whatever our published standard for that
      product is — a botanical wood, resin, herb, or flower, not a synthetic fragrance unless stated —
      and it is held to the same quality-control process as every other order. A buyer who wants to
      verify what is actually in the product can do so against the
      <a href="/ingredients/">ingredient encyclopedia</a> and the
      <a href="/blog/incense-authentication-database/">authentication database</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>What private label is not</h2>
    <p>
      Three distinctions keep the term precise, and each prevents an expensive misunderstanding:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Not OEM.</strong> Private label re-brands a product that already exists; OEM builds a
        product to your specification. If you are expecting a custom formula, private label is the
        wrong door.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Not custom fragrance development.</strong> There is no brief, no formulation, and no
        iteration loop. You are selecting, not commissioning.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Not necessarily lower quality.</strong> "Existing product" describes <em>when</em> the
        product was made, not <em>how well</em>. The material and quality control are whatever the
        manufacturer's standard is — which is exactly what you verify before ordering.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Who private label is for</h2>
    <p>
      Private label fits a specific buyer: someone who has a <strong>brand, a channel, and a
      market</strong> — but no product team. That typically means:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>A retailer, marketplace seller, or distributor who wants a house brand on a proven product fast.</li>
      <li>A brand testing a new channel or region without the cost of developing a product first.</li>
      <li>A buyer whose market is already served by an existing product and who only needs labeling and packaging.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If, instead, you have a specific scent, ingredient, or format in mind — a product that does not
      yet exist — you need <a href="/oem/">OEM</a>. The choice is one question:
      <em>"do I have a product, or only a brand?"</em>
    </p>

    <h2>How we work</h2>
    <p>
      Because the product already exists, the private-label path is short. The steps are:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Select the product.</strong> You choose from existing formats and materials. This is
        the step that makes private label fast — there is no formula development.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sampling.</strong> You evaluate the product against your expectations before committing.
        A sample verifies the product — aroma, format, material, and burn — not the supplier's delivery
        at volume; that is what a trial order is for. See
        <a href="/blog/samples-program/">the samples program</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Production.</strong> The approved product is produced to your order quantity, held to
        the reference sample through the three-stage quality-control process in
        <a href="/blog/incense-manufacturing-quality-control/">manufacturing quality control</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Quality control.</strong> Incoming inspection, in-process control, and final inspection
        against the agreed specification — the same process every order passes through.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Packaging.</strong> Your label, box, and any destination-required marks are applied. See
        <a href="/blog/packaging-shipping-guide/">packaging &amp; shipping</a>.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What buyers provide</h2>
    <p>
      In private label, the buyer's contribution is the <em>brand</em>, not the product. What we need
      from you:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Your brand and label artwork</strong> — the name, logo, and any text that goes on the product.</li>
      <li><strong>Packaging requirements</strong> — bulk versus retail-ready, box spec, and any destination labeling.</li>
      <li><strong>Quantity and destination</strong> — the target order size and where it ships, which drive every quotation.</li>
      <li><strong>Any specification you need to confirm</strong> — for example, that the existing product's material and burn meet your market's expectations.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The brand is yours; the formula is not. Formula ownership stays with the manufacturer in a
      private-label arrangement — you own the brand you put on the product, not the recipe behind it.
      This is a point worth being clear about up front, because it differs from
      <a href="/oem/">OEM</a>, where formula ownership is a contract term to settle in writing.
    </p>

    <h2>What you control — and what you don't</h2>
    <p>
      Private label splits control cleanly. What is yours: the <strong>brand name, logo, label
      design, packaging, and how the product is presented</strong> to your market. What is not yours:
      the <strong>formula, the material specification, and the production process</strong> — those stay
      with the manufacturer. That division is the whole point of the route: you bring the brand, the
      manufacturer brings the product, and each side controls what it is good at.
    </p>
    <p>
      The practical consequence is that two buyers can sell the same underlying product under two
      different labels and differentiate on brand, packaging, and channel rather than on formula. If
      that shared-product reality is a problem — if you need a product your competitors cannot simply
      re-brand — that is a reason to move toward <a href="/oem/">OEM</a>, where the formula itself is
      yours to direct.
    </p>

    <h2>What we confirm</h2>
    <p>
      Because specifications vary by product and order, we confirm the following per inquiry rather than
      publishing fixed figures: <strong>MOQ, lead time, packaging options, certifications, and shipping
      terms.</strong> There is no published price list for private label — like every serious quotation,
      it is prepared per product, quantity, and destination. See
      <a href="/blog/moq-pricing-guide/">MOQ &amp; pricing</a> for why "on request" is the honest
      answer, not an evasion.
    </p>
    <p>
      On documentation specifically, we treat certificates and test reports as claims to verify, not
      badges to assume. We request and provide documents per order and verify them against the issuing
      body — the same discipline we recommend buyers apply to any supplier in the
      <a href="/blog/factory-vetting-guide/">factory vetting guide</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Private label vs OEM, plainly</h2>
    <p>
      The two terms are often used interchangeably, but they solve different problems:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Dimension</th>
          <th>Private label</th>
          <th>OEM</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Product</td>
          <td>Existing product</td>
          <td>Built to your specification</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Your contribution</td>
          <td>Brand and packaging</td>
          <td>Brief, formula direction, and brand</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Lead time</td>
          <td>Shorter (production only)</td>
          <td>Longer (development + production)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Control</td>
          <td>Medium — label, not formula</td>
          <td>High — formula and direction</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Best for</td>
          <td>A brand without a product team</td>
          <td>A specific scent or format in mind</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Neither is "better." If you have a brand and a market and want to launch quickly on a proven
      product, private label is the right fit. If you need a product that does not exist yet, you need
      <a href="/oem/">OEM</a>. The full treatment, with the questions you must ask, is in the
      <a href="/blog/oem-vs-private-label/">OEM vs private label comparison</a>, the
      <a href="/blog/oem-private-label-guide/">OEM &amp; private-label guide</a>, and the
      <a href="/incense-manufacturer/">incense manufacturer</a> page.
    </p>

